      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-1\\pm 3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.54$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.54$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14130022/assets/30d1e296e7c83fa8925feb116c9035de14790e89ddad37ab44710feacadf13a8.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14130022/assets/054b319170c634494c703d9588419cb6e44414196c970ca0c39689bc62f90d97.png)",
